{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 38, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli (c. 1140 – 1187) was the last ruler from the dynasty of the Crusader state of the County of Tripoli. He inherited the title at the age of 11 following the death of his father Raymond II in 1152. His mother Hodierna of Jerusalem served as regent during his minority. Raymond III’s reign was marked by the political intrigues and military challenges that plagued the Crusader states in the 12th century.\n\n*Highlighted section part 1*\n**Early years and regency**\nHodierna managed Tripoli’s affairs with an iron fist ensuring stability while fending off external threats. She secured alliances with neighboring states like the Principality of Antioch and the Kingdom of Jerusalem. Her rule was characterized by pragmatism and diplomacy which laid the groundwork for Raymond III’s future governance. By the time he came of age Raymond III was poised to take control of a relatively strong Tripoli though tensions with Muslim powers particularly with the Burid dynasty in Damascus remained a looming concern.\n\n**Marriage and political alliances**\nIn 1164 Raymond III married Eschiva of Bures daughter of the Lord of Bures in France. The union was part of a broader strategy to strengthen ties with Western Europe. Eschiva played a significant role in supporting her husband’s political ambitions and the couple had two children a son who died young and a daughter Maria of Tripoli. Raymond’s political maneuvering included aligning himself with key figures in the Crusader states such as the powerful Templars and Hospitallers whose military and financial backing were crucial for defending against Muslim advances.\n\n**Military conflict and the fall of Tripoli**\nRaymond III’s reign was dominated by the struggle against the Muslimpowers particularly Nur ad-Din and later his successor Saladin. In 1167 Tripoli faced a siege by Nur ad-Din’s forces but withstood the attack thanks to the efforts of Hodierna and the military resources of the Crusader states. However Raymond’s relationship with Baldwin IV of Jerusalem deteriorated due to personal conflicts and differing strategies for dealing with the Muslim threat. This rivalry weakened the Crusader coalition making Tripoli increasingly vulnerable. By 1187 Saladin’s forces amassed a large army and besieged Tripoli. Raymond III chose to send his wife and daughter to safety in Antioch while he and his troops prepared for the inevitable assault. After a fierce but hopeless defense the city fell to Saladin in July 1187 marking the end of Tripoli as an independent Crusader state. Raymond III was captured and imprisoned in Damascus where he died shortly after his capture.\n\n**Legacy and family**\nRaymond III’s death in captivity effectively ended the independent line of the Counts of Tripoli. His widow Eschiva briefly held the title of Countess in name but Tripoli became a vassal of Saladin and later the Ayyubid dynasty. Raymond III’s story reflects the broader challenges faced by the Crusader states during the 12th century as they struggled to maintain their footholds in the Levant against increasingly powerful and unified Muslim forces. His reign highlighted the importance of alliances both within the Crusader states and with external powers as well as the critical role of military strength and diplomacy in preserving these fragile kingdoms. His capture and death symbolized the ultimate fate of many Crusader strongholds in the face of the rising Ayyubid caliphate.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.06853077560663223}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.00012003655137959868}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.006313735153526068}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.29494979977607727}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00038413776201196015}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -0.01811952330172062}, {"token": " (", "logprob": -3.9808387756347656}, {"token": "c